I first downloaded NetHack about six weeks ago, the day 3.4.3 came
out. I was immediately hooked, and it has consumed far too much of my
life in the interim (including over half of my Christmas break). Last
week I got to Medusa's swamp for the first time. It was a disaster.

My friend Nolan says that there are three kinds of deaths in NetHack:
when one is overly careless, when one is overly greedy, and when one
is simply smote by the whims of the RNG. This was mostly a case of the
first, although I maintain it had elements of the third.

Mia the Valkyrie was gifted with Mjollnir and Vorpal Blade, both very
well-enchanted, GDSM from a magic lamp (one of only two lamps in the
first twenty-two levels of the game, although she wasn't wanting for
brass lanterns), and gauntlets of power for the ever-so-enjoyable
pursuit of chucking enormous lightning-invoking hammers at bad guys.
The trip down to Medusa was uneventful, save for the incident
involving a spiked--poisoned spiked!--pit and a fortuitously-worn
"oLS.

Anyhow, Mia gets to Medusa and realizes she has no way of getting
across the swamp. No levitation, no water-walking boots, no TC, nada.
She has a /oC, but it craps out short of her goal, and with the amount
of metal armour she's wearing, swimming isn't an option.

She trucks back up to dungeon level 1 and very methodically makes her
way back down, looking for anything that'll get her over the swamp.
(At this point I should mention that she wasn't looking with any kind
of enthusaism: she hadn't seen a single blindfold or towel in the
entire game, and wasn't going to trust that diluted potion of
blindness with her life. Reflection--or rather, the lack thereof--was
an issue, too; she had a number of looking glasses, but no amulet or
armour to instantly petrify the big M. She didn't regret picking the
G- over the SDSM; she'd lost track of the number of polymorph traps
and wands of death that'd saved her from.)

In twenty-three levels of dungeon and however-many of Gnomish Mines
(Sokoban was a washout--and no, I don't want to talk about it), in
easily a dozen suitable shops, there was not a single method for
getting across that double-damned swamp. Lacking in self-blinding
methods anyway, she decided to cut her losses and dig past level 23
entirely: maybe there'd be something useful later, and she could come
back and deal with Miss Clairol's worst nightmare then.

Navigating the post-Medusa mazes was trying, but after a little trying
she got down to level 25. Realizing simultaneously that a) she'd been
saving a potion of object detection for a rainy day, and b) it never
rains underground, she thought she'd go back up and give twenty-four
another once-over, this time with the help of an emerald potion.

Sigh. Mia. You really should have been paying closer attention. That
wasn't level 25 you were heading up from after all; it was level 24.
Before she knew what was happening--hell, before *I* knew what was
happening--she'd come up into Medusa's chamber, met the Gorgon's gaze,
and turned to stone.

